Sure, here are five different programming languages calculating the Fibonacci sequence up to the 1024th number:

### Python
```python
def fibonacci(n):
    a, b = 0, 1
    for _ in range(n):
        a, b = b, a + b
    return a

print(fibonacci(1024))
```

### JavaScript
```javascript
function fibonacci(n) {
    let a = 0, b = 1;
    while (n-- > 0) [a, b] = [b, a + b];
    return a;
}

console.log(fibonacci(1024));
```

### Java
```java
public class Fibonacci {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 1024;
        long a = 0, b = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            long temp = a + b;
            a = b;
            b = temp;
        }
        System.out.println(a);
    }
}
```

### C++
```cpp
#include <iostream>

long fibonacci(int n) {
    long a = 0, b = 1;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        long temp = a + b;
        a = b;
        b = temp;
    }
    return a;
}

int main() {
    std::cout << fibonacci(1024) << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

### Rust
```rust
fn fibonacci(n: usize) -> u64 {
    let mut a = 0;
    let mut b = 1;
    for _ in 0..n {
        let temp = a + b;
        a = b;
        b = temp;
    }
    a
}

fn main() {
    println!("{}", fibonacci(1024));
}
```

These examples demonstrate how to calculate the Fibonacci sequence up to the 1024th number in Python, JavaScript, Java, C++, and Rust.
